\title[A precision-halving law for sum-and-product hints]{The Routing Is Dial-Dependent, but Not Through Residues Mod $8$:\\ A Precision-Halving Law for Sum-and-Product Hints}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{September 25, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study how much information about the prime factors of an RSA-type modulus $N=pq$ is revealed by a partial \emph{sum hint} $s=p+q$ reduced modulo a prime power. The problem arose from an experiment on \emph{dials}: prime-indexed splitting-type maps attached to number fields with Galois groups $S_3$, $A_4$, $D_4$, $F_{20}$ and $C_5$. In that experiment the sum/difference information routing turned out to depend on the dial. The dyadic dial $D_4@8$ was \emph{sum-sufficient}, while the $S_3$ dials required sum and difference together. The explanation proposed for the $D_4$ behaviour was that ``$(p+q)\bmod 8$ determines $p\bmod 8$ and $q \bmod 8$ uniquely.'' We prove this is false. The ordered version fails for every $N$. The unordered version holds only for $N\equiv 5\pmod 8$. For $N\equiv 3\pmod 4$ the sum modulo $8$ carries no information at all. The primes $17\cdot 41$ and $5\cdot 13$ give an explicit counterexample. We also show that the accompanying contrast with Legendre symbols is backwards. In place of the mod-$8$ explanation we prove a \emph{precision law}, valid for every prime $\ell$: agreement of sum and product modulo $\ell^k$ forces agreement of the unordered factor pairs modulo $\ell^{\ceilhalf{k}}$, and this is sharp. In the separated (Hensel) regime $\ell\nmid p-q$ there is no loss, but odd factors are never separated at $\ell=2$. For $D_4@8$ it follows that sum and $N$ modulo $32$ determine the unordered type pair, while modulo $16$ they do not.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Hints, dials and routing}
Let $N=pq$ be a product of two distinct odd primes, and put $s=p+q$ and $d=q-p$. Then $d^2=s^2-4N$, and $p,q$ are the roots of $X^2-sX+N$ (Vieta). Exact knowledge of $s$ therefore factors $N$. The interesting question is what \emph{partial} information reveals.

A \emph{dial} is a map $\tau$ from primes (outside a finite set) to a finite set of \emph{types}. In the examples of interest, $\tau(p)$ is the splitting type of $p$ in a fixed number field, and the dial is labelled by a Galois group and a modulus or conductor: $S_3@23$, $S_3@31$, $A_4@9$, $D_4@8$, $F_{20}@5$ and $C_5@11$. For $D_4@8$ the field lies in $\Q(\zeta_8)$ and $\tau(p)$ depends only on $p\bmod 8$.

The routing experiment measures how much information about the unordered type pair $\{\tau(p),\tau(q)\}$ is carried by the sum channel alone, by the difference channel alone, and synergistically:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrl}
\toprule
dial & $s$-carried & $d$-carried & $s$--$d$ synergy & structure\\
\midrule
$S_3@31$ & $4.0\%$ & $3.8\%$ & $+1.44$ & combination required\\
$S_3@23$ & $5.2\%$ & $5.1\%$ & $+1.41$ & combination required\\
$A_4@9$ & $161.6\%$ & $213.9\%$ & $+0.01$ & noise on near-zero channel\\
$D_4@8$ & $100.0\%$ & $75.2\%$ & $-1.00$ & sum-sufficient\\
$F_{20}@5$ & $165.0\%$ & $122.2\%$ & $+0.41$ & both exceed\\
$C_5@11$ & $77.8\%$ & $55.7\%$ & $+1.18$ & combination required\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

An earlier hypothesis held that the ``combination required'' pattern is universal. The table refutes it: the routing is dial-dependent. We accept this verdict. Our subject is its explanation.

\subsection{The proposed explanation and what we prove}
The proposed explanation was:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(E1)] $D_4@8$ is sum-sufficient because its type map is $f(p\bmod 8)$, and $(p+q)\bmod 8$ determines $p\bmod 8$ and $q\bmod 8$ uniquely, via $q\equiv Np^{-1}\pmod 8$.
\item[(E2)] $S_3$ fields require both residues because $(\Delta\mid p)$ is not determined by $(\Delta\mid p)+(\Delta\mid q)$.
\end{itemize}
We show that (E1) is false in every reading (Section~\ref{sec:mod8}) and that (E2) is backwards (Proposition~\ref{prop:signs}). We then prove a sharp precision law for all primes (Section~\ref{sec:law}) and derive the correct $D_4@8$ threshold (Section~\ref{sec:d4}).

\section{Notation and definitions}
Throughout, $\ell$ is a prime, $k\ge 0$, and $\ceilhalf{k}=\lfloor (k+1)/2\rfloor$.

\begin{definition}[Unordered agreement]
Pairs $(p,q)$ and $(p',q')$ \emph{agree as unordered pairs modulo $m$} if ($p\equiv p'$ and $q\equiv q'$) or ($p\equiv q'$ and $q\equiv p'$) modulo $m$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Factor swap and pair sum]
Let $U=(\Z/8)^\times=\{1,3,5,7\}$. For $c\in U$ (the class of $N$) and $u\in U$ (the class of one factor), the \emph{partner class} is $\sigma_c(u)=cu^{-1}$, and the \emph{pair sum} is $\Sigma_c(u)=u+\sigma_c(u)\in\Z/8$.
\end{definition}

If $p\equiv u$ and $pq\equiv c\pmod 8$, then $p+q\equiv \Sigma_c(u)\pmod 8$. So $\Sigma_c$ is exactly what a sum hint modulo $8$ shows, given $N \bmod 8$.

\begin{definition}[Sum-sufficiency modulo $8$]
The class $c$ is \emph{ordered sum-sufficient} if $\Sigma_c$ is injective on $U$. It is \emph{unordered sum-sufficient} if $\Sigma_c(u)=\Sigma_c(v)$ implies $v=u$ or $v=\sigma_c(u)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Separated pair]
A pair $(p,q)$ is \emph{$\ell$-separated} if $\ell\nmid p-q$.
\end{definition}

\section{The mod-$8$ explanation is false}\label{sec:mod8}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:inv}
Every $u\in U$ satisfies $u^{-1}=u$; that is, $U\cong C_2\times C_2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$1^2$, $3^2=9$, $5^2=25$ and $7^2=49$ are all $\equiv 1\pmod 8$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Pair-sum formula]\label{lem:pairsum}
For all $c,u\in U$ we have $\Sigma_c(u)=u(1+c)$ in $\Z/8$, and $4(1+c)=0$ in $\Z/8$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:inv}, $\sigma_c(u)=cu$, so $\Sigma_c(u)=u+cu=u(1+c)$. Since $c$ is odd, $1+c$ is even, and hence $4(1+c)\equiv 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ccccc}
\toprule
$c=N\bmod 8$ & $u=1$ & $u=3$ & $u=5$ & $u=7$\\
\midrule
$1$ & $2$ & $6$ & $2$ & $6$\\
$3$ & $4$ & $4$ & $4$ & $4$\\
$5$ & $6$ & $2$ & $6$ & $2$\\
$7$ & $0$ & $0$ & $0$ & $0$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{theorem}[Shift invariance; ordered claim false for every $N$]\label{thm:shift}
For all $c,u\in U$, $\Sigma_c(5u)=\Sigma_c(u)$ and $5u\neq u$. Consequently, for every $c$ there exist $u\neq v$ in $U$ with $\Sigma_c(u)=\Sigma_c(v)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $5=1+4$. Then $\Sigma_c(5u)=\Sigma_c(u)+u\cdot 4(1+c)=\Sigma_c(u)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:pairsum}. Also $5u-u=4u\neq 0$ since $u$ is odd.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Zero information for $N\equiv 3\pmod 4$]\label{thm:zero}
If $c\in\{3,7\}$ then $\Sigma_c$ is constant on $U$: equal to $4$ for $c=3$ and to $0$ for $c=7$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$1+c\in\{4,8\}$, and $4u\equiv 4$ and $8u\equiv 0 \pmod 8$ for odd $u$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Classification]\label{thm:class}
$c\in U$ is unordered sum-sufficient if and only if $c=5$. In particular exactly one of the four classes of $N\bmod 8$ is sum-sufficient.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $c=5$ the fibres of $\Sigma_5$ are $\{1,5\}$ and $\{3,7\}$, and $\sigma_5$ swaps $1\leftrightarrow 5$ and $3\leftrightarrow 7$. For $c=1$, $\sigma_1=\mathrm{id}$ and $\Sigma_1(1)=\Sigma_1(5)$. For $c\in\{3,7\}$, $\Sigma_c$ is constant, and any $v\notin\{1,c\}$ violates sufficiency.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Prime counterexample]\label{prop:primes}
The numbers $17,41,5,13$ are prime. Moreover $17\cdot 41\equiv 5\cdot 13$ and $17+41\equiv 5+13\pmod 8$, while $17\not\equiv 5$ and $17\not\equiv 13\pmod 8$, and $17\cdot 41\not\equiv 5\cdot 13\pmod{32}$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$697\equiv 65\equiv 1$ and $58\equiv 18\equiv 2\pmod 8$. The factor classes are $\{1,1\}$ and $\{5,5\}$. Finally $697\equiv 25$ and $65\equiv 1\pmod{32}$.
\end{proof}

A prime $\equiv 1\pmod 8$ splits completely in $\Q(\zeta_8)$ and a prime $\equiv 5\pmod 8$ does not. The two semiprimes thus have identical data $(N\bmod 8,s\bmod 8)=(1,2)$ but different $D_4@8$ type pairs.

\begin{proposition}[The symbol contrast, corrected]\label{prop:signs}
If $a,b,a',b'\in\{\pm 1\}$ and $a+b=a'+b'$, then $(a,b)=(a',b')$ or $(a,b)=(b',a')$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The sums $2,0,-2$ correspond exactly to the unordered pairs $\{1,1\},\{1,-1\},\{-1,-1\}$.
\end{proof}

\section{The precision law}\label{sec:law}

\begin{lemma}[Valuation pigeonhole]\label{lem:pigeon}
Let $\ell$ be a prime and $a,b\ge 0$. If $\ell^{a+b+1}\mid xy$, then $\ell^{a+1}\mid x$ or $\ell^{b+1}\mid y$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $a$. If $\ell\nmid x$, then $\ell^{a+b+1}$ is coprime to $x$, so $\ell^{a+b+1}\mid y$. If $\ell\mid x$ and $a=0$ we are done. Otherwise write $x=\ell x'$, cancel one factor of $\ell$, and apply the induction hypothesis to $\ell^{(a-1)+b+1}\mid x'y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Vieta identity]\label{lem:vieta}
In any commutative ring,
\[(p-p')(p-q')=p\bigl[(p+q)-(p'+q')\bigr]+\bigl[p'q'-pq\bigr].\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Direct expansion.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Precision law]\label{thm:law}
Let $\ell$ be a prime, $k\ge 0$, and suppose $p+q\equiv p'+q'$ and $pq\equiv p'q'\pmod{\ell^k}$. Then $(p,q)$ and $(p',q')$ agree as unordered pairs modulo $\ell^{\ceilhalf{k}}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $c=\ceilhalf{k}$. The case $c=0$ is trivial. Otherwise write $c=a+1$, so that $2a+1\le k$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:vieta}, $\ell^{a+a+1}$ divides $(p-p')(p-q')$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:pigeon}, $\ell^c\mid p-p'$ or $\ell^c\mid p-q'$. In the first case $q-q'=[(p+q)-(p'+q')]-(p-p')$ is divisible by $\ell^c$, since $c\le k$. The second case is symmetric.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]\label{thm:sharp}
Let $\ell$ be a prime and $c\ge 0$. Put $p=1$, $q=1-2\ell^c$, $p'=q'=1-\ell^c$. Then $p+q=p'+q'$, $pq\equiv p'q'\pmod{\ell^{2c}}$, and neither $p\equiv p'$ nor $p\equiv q'\pmod{\ell^{c+1}}$. For $\ell=2$ and $c\ge 1$ all four numbers are odd.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both sums equal $2-2\ell^c$. Also $p'q'-pq=\ell^{2c}$, and $p-p'=\ell^c$ is not divisible by $\ell^{c+1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Taking $c=\ceilhalf{k}$, so that $2c \ge k$, shows that the exponent in Theorem~\ref{thm:law} is optimal for every prime and every $k$. Exhaustive enumeration over residue pairs confirms that the determined precision is exactly $\ceilhalf{k}$ for $\ell=2$, $k\le 7$; $\ell=3$, $k\le 4$; and $\ell=5$, $k\le 3$. Heuristically, the roots $\tfrac12(s\pm\sqrt{s^2-4N})$ nearly coincide when the discriminant is highly divisible by $\ell$, and a perturbation of size $\ell^k$ moves them by about $\ell^{k/2}$.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Exact Vieta]\label{thm:exact}
In an integral domain, if $p+q=p'+q'$ and $pq=p'q'$, then $(p,q)=(p',q')$ or $(p,q)=(q',p')$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:vieta}, $(p-p')(p-q')=0$. Hence $p=p'$ or $p=q'$, and the sum gives the other coordinate.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Hensel regime]\label{thm:hensel}
Let $\ell$ be a prime and suppose $\ell\nmid p-q$. If $p+q\equiv p'+q'$ and $pq\equiv p'q'\pmod{\ell^k}$, then $(p,q)$ and $(p',q')$ agree as unordered pairs modulo $\ell^k$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $k\ge 1$, $\ell^k\mid (p-p')(p-q')$. If $\ell$ divided both factors, then $\ell$ would divide $p-q=(p-p')+(p-q')-[(p+q)-(p'+q')]$. So one factor is prime to $\ell$, and $\ell^k$ divides the other. Conclude as in Theorem~\ref{thm:law}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Dyadic obstruction]\label{prop:dyadic}
If $p,q$ are odd then $2\mid p-q$. Odd pairs are therefore never $2$-separated.
\end{proposition}

\section{Consequences for the $D_4@8$ dial}\label{sec:d4}

The $D_4@8$ type of $p$ depends on $p\bmod 2^3$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:law} requires $\ceilhalf{k}\ge 3$, that is $k\ge 5$.

\begin{corollary}[Modulus $32$ suffices]\label{cor:32}
If $p+q\equiv p'+q'$ and $pq\equiv p'q'\pmod{32}$, then $(p,q)$ and $(p',q')$ agree as unordered pairs modulo $8$, and therefore have the same unordered $D_4@8$ type pair.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Modulus $16$ does not]\label{cor:16}
We have $1+9\equiv 13+13$ and $1\cdot 9\equiv 13\cdot 13\pmod{16}$, but $1\not\equiv 13$ and $9\not\equiv 13\pmod 8$. The classes mod $8$ are $\{1,1\}$ and $\{5,5\}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}[Numerical illustration]
For $3000$ random semiprimes with $20$-bit factors, the fraction whose unordered type pair $\{p\bmod 8,q\bmod 8\}$ is pinned within the sample by $(s,N)\bmod 2^k$ was about $24\%$ for $k=3$, $75\%$ for $k=4$, and $100\%$ for all $k\ge 5$. The values below $k = 5$ are empirical; the value at $k \ge 5$ is guaranteed by Corollary~\ref{cor:32}.
\end{remark}

The measured $100\%$ sum-carried fraction for $D_4@8$ is therefore incompatible with a channel that reveals only $s\bmod 8$. It must come from sum information modulo $32$ or finer, for example the exact integer sum.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithm}[Pair-sum classifier modulo $8$]
Given $c=N\bmod 8$, compute $\Sigma_c(u)=u(1+c)\bmod 8$ and the partner $cu\bmod 8$ for $u\in U$. Group the $u$ by $\Sigma_c(u)$. Return ``sufficient'' iff each group is contained in $\{u,cu\}$. Cost $O(1)$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:class} the output is ``sufficient'' iff $c=5$.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Determined-precision oracle]
Given $\ell,k$, enumerate $0\le p\le q<\ell^k$ (optionally only separated pairs), bucket them by $(p+q,pq)\bmod\ell^k$, and return the largest $j\le k$ such that every bucket has a single image among unordered pairs modulo $\ell^j$. Cost $O(k\,\ell^{2k})$. The output is $\ceilhalf{k}$ unfiltered and $k$ on separated pairs.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Unordered pair recovery]
Given $(s,n)\bmod\ell^k$ coming from a true pair, find any $p\bmod\ell^k$ with $p^2-sp+n\equiv 0\pmod{\ell^k}$, and output $\{p,s-p\}\bmod\ell^{\ceilhalf{k}}$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:law} the output does not depend on which solution is found. When the true pair is separated, the output is correct modulo $\ell^k$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:hensel}.
\end{algorithm}

\section{Discussion}
The empirical verdict that routing is dial-dependent survives. Both parts of the structural explanation fail. (E1) confuses determination by sum and product at sufficient precision with determination by the residue of the sum modulo $8$. It is defeated by the exponent-two structure of $(\Z/8)^\times$. (E2) fails because a symmetric sum of signs is injective on unordered pairs. The replacement is a two-regime picture. At an odd prime, off the diagonal, sum and product are complete to full precision. At $\ell=2$, for odd factors, they are complete only to half precision. The difference between $D_4@8$ and odd-conductor dials may thus be a difference between dyadic and odd moduli, rather than between Galois groups.

\section{Future work}
\begin{conjecture}[Precision-halving for the measured $D_4$ channel]
If the $D_4@8$ experiment receives only $s\bmod 2^k$, the $s$-carried fraction is below $100\%$ for $k\le 4$ and exactly $100\%$ for $k\ge 5$.
\end{conjecture}
\begin{conjecture}[Hensel dichotomy]
At $S_3@23$, $S_3@31$, $C_5@11$ and $F_{20}@5$, the $s$--$d$ synergy comes only from pairs with $p\equiv q\pmod\ell$, a set of density about $1/\ell$.
\end{conjecture}
\begin{conjecture}[Galois-independent routing criterion]
Routing depends on the dial modulus through its $2$-adic part and the proportion of ramified classes, not on the Galois group. A $D_4$ dial at an odd conductor, or an $S_3$ dial at a $2$-power modulus, would distinguish the two readings.
\end{conjecture}
\begin{conjecture}[Difference-channel square-root law]
Since $d^2=s^2-4N$, the channel $d\bmod 2^k$ gives the pair modulo $2^{\lceil (k-1)/2\rceil}$, and $(s,d)\bmod 2^k$ jointly gives $p$ modulo $2^{k-1}$ (as $2p=s-d$).
\end{conjecture}

